## ⚙️ The Three Eras of Modern Java — What Each One Solved

Java 8 to Java 25 covers eleven years and three distinct architectural shifts. Each era solves a specific category of pain that was previously handled by boilerplate, convention, or workarounds. The dedicated posts below go deep on each era with full before/after code pairs, internals, and migration guides.

### Era 1 (Java 8–11): Lambdas, Streams, and the LTS Baseline

Java 8 ended the anonymous-inner-class era. Lambdas made functions first-class values. The Streams API replaced imperative for-loops with declarative pipelines. `Optional<T>` made null-safety explicit at the return type. Java 9's module system made dependency boundaries compiler-enforceable — though most teams skip it unless building minimal runtime images with `jlink`. Java 10 added `var` for local type inference. Java 11 rounded out the baseline with `strip()`, `isBlank()`, `HttpClient`, and `Files.readString()` — it became the new Java 8.

```java
// Java 7 — 6-line Comparator just to sort a list
Collections.sort(names, new Comparator<String>() {
    @Override public int compare(String a, String b) { return a.compareTo(b); }
});

// Java 8+ — method reference; one token
names.sort(String::compareTo);
```

**→ Full deep-dive: [Java 8 to 11: Lambdas, Streams, and the Module System](./java-8-to-11-lambdas-streams-modules)**

### Era 2 (Java 14–17): Records, Sealed Classes, and Pattern Matching

The Java 14–17 era is defined by language ergonomics. Records replaced 40–60-line POJOs with a single-line declaration — the compiler generates the constructor, accessors, `equals()`, `hashCode()`, and `toString()`. Text blocks made embedded SQL, JSON, and HTML readable without escape sequences. Sealed classes brought algebraic data types to Java's type system — no more comment-only hierarchy restrictions. Pattern matching for `instanceof` eliminated the redundant cast ceremony. Java 17 LTS made all of these stable and production-ready.

```java
// Java 15 and below — 50+ lines of POJO ceremony
public final class Point { /* constructor, getX(), getY(), equals(), hashCode(), toString() */ }

// Java 16+ — compiler generates everything
public record Point(int x, int y) {}
```

**→ Full deep-dive: [Java 14 to 17: Records, Sealed Classes, and Pattern Matching](./java-14-to-17-records-sealed-pattern-matching)**

### Era 3 (Java 21–25): Virtual Threads and Modern Concurrency

Java 21 is the most impactful release since Java 8. Virtual threads replace the bounded-thread-pool model for I/O-bound work: every task gets its own virtual thread, the JVM mounts and unmounts them on a small pool of carrier OS threads, and blocking I/O never occupies an OS thread. Pattern matching for `switch` with sealed classes gives compiler-checked exhaustive dispatch — no more `default` branches to paper over unhandled cases. Sequenced collections add `getFirst()` and `getLast()` to every ordered collection. Java 25 promotes structured concurrency to GA, adds scoped values, and introduces unnamed variables (`_`).

```java
// Java 8 — pool hard limit; request 51 waits in queue
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(50);

// Java 21+ — one virtual thread per task; JVM schedules thousands concurrently
ExecutorService vt = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();
```

**→ Full deep-dive: [Java 21 to 25: Virtual Threads, Pattern Matching, and Modern Concurrency](./java-21-to-25-virtual-threads-pattern-matching)**

