## ⚖️ Trade-offs & Failure Modes: When OCP Is Over-Engineering

OCP adds indirection. That's only worth the cost when variation is genuinely expected.

| Scenario | Apply OCP | Skip OCP |
|---|---|---|
| Multiple implementations of the same behavior (e.g., payment types) | ✅ | — |
| Behavior that varies by external configuration or runtime | ✅ | — |
| Single implementation that will never vary | — | ✅ |
| Early-stage prototyping where requirements are unclear | — | ✅ |
| One-off scripts or CLI tools | — | ✅ |

**The trap:** abstracting too early. If you build an interface for a class that only ever has one implementation, you've added complexity with no benefit. OCP is reactive — apply it when the second variant appears, or when you are confident variation is coming.

